\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

We present OntoCoDec, a two-layer constrained decoding framework that compiles ontology schemas into token-level masks enforced during autoregressive generation.
The grammar layer (\S\ref{sec:grammar}) guarantees structural validity---well-formed JSON with ontology-vocabulary membership---while the semantic layer (\S\ref{sec:semantic}) enforces domain/range type constraints via a stateful entity registry.
Formal analysis of constraint composition is deferred to \S\ref{sec:composition}.


\subsection{Problem Formulation}
\label{sec:formulation}

\paragraph{IE as structured generation.}
Given input text $x$, a language model generates a JSON output $y$:
%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:output-schema}
\resizebox{.88\columnwidth}{!}{$\displaystyle
  y = \bigl\{\texttt{entities}: [\{n_i, \tau_i\}],\; \texttt{relations}: [\{h_j, t_j, r_j, \tau^h_j, \tau^t_j\}]\bigr\}
$}
\end{equation}
%
where each entity has name $n_i$ and type $\tau_i$, and each relation specifies head $h_j$, tail $t_j$, relation type $r_j$, and head/tail types $\tau^h_j, \tau^t_j$.

\paragraph{Ontology compliance.}
Given $\mathcal{O} = (\mathcal{E}, \mathcal{R}, \operatorname{dom}, \operatorname{range})$, a triple $(h, r, t)$ is \emph{type-valid} iff:
%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:type-valid}
  \tau^h \in \operatorname{dom}(r) \;\wedge\; \tau^t \in \operatorname{range}(r).
\end{equation}
%
We define the \emph{domain/range violation rate} (DR) as the fraction of triples failing Eq.~\eqref{eq:type-valid}.
Our objective is a decoding strategy that maximizes $P(y \mid x)$ subject to structural well-formedness and type validity under $\mathcal{O}$.


\subsection{Grammar Layer}
\label{sec:grammar}

The grammar layer compiles $\mathcal{O}$ into a context-free grammar enforced at every decoding step via logit masking.

\paragraph{Schema-to-grammar compilation.}
We generate a JSON Schema encoding the structure in Eq.~\eqref{eq:output-schema}, with \texttt{enum} constraints restricting entity types to $\mathcal{E}$ and relation types to $\mathcal{R}$.
XGrammar~\cite{dong2025xgrammar} compiles this into a pushdown automaton tracking JSON nesting, field names, string boundaries, and enum membership.

\paragraph{Logit masking.}
At each step $t$, the grammar processor computes grammar-valid tokens $G(t) \subseteq V$ from the automaton state, masking all others to $-\infty$.
This enforces: (i)~\emph{structural validity}---well-formed JSON with correct fields; (ii)~\emph{relation vocabulary}---$r_j \in \mathcal{R}$; and (iii)~\emph{entity type membership}---$\tau_i \in \mathcal{E}$.
Domain/range constraints are not captured, since they depend on per-triple entity types available only during generation---the gap filled by the semantic layer.

% exp_ids: phase2_scierc_grammar_only (RV 1.39→0%, ET 0.40→0%, DR 15.37→14.84%, R-F1 8.23)
\paragraph{Empirical profile.}
On SciERC, grammar-only constraints eliminate RV (1.39\%\,$\to$\,0\%) and ET (0.40\%\,$\to$\,0\%) violations entirely with no F1 impact.
DR remains unchanged (15.37\%\,$\to$\,14.84\%), confirming that grammar enforces structural but not semantic validity.


\subsection{Semantic Layer}
\label{sec:semantic}

The semantic layer enforces domain/range constraints through a stateful logits processor.

\paragraph{Entity registry.}
\label{sec:entity-registry}
As entities are generated, the layer builds a registry $\mathcal{D}: \text{name} \to \text{type}$ mapping each entity to its declared type.
When the model completes an entry $\{n_i, \tau_i\}$, the pair is added to $\mathcal{D}$ and consulted during subsequent relation generation.

\paragraph{Position detection.}
\label{sec:position-detection}
At each step $t$, the processor determines the active field from $\mathcal{F} = \{\textsc{EntityType},$ $\textsc{RelationType},$ $\textsc{HeadType},$ $\textsc{TailType},$ $\textsc{FreeText}\}$ by pattern-matching on the decoded prefix.
Semantic masking activates only at type-bearing positions (\textsc{HeadType}, \textsc{TailType}).

\paragraph{Dynamic masking.}
\label{sec:dynamic-masking}
At a \textsc{HeadType} position, if the head entity $h_j \in \mathcal{D}$ and $\mathcal{D}[h_j] \in \operatorname{dom}(r_j)$, the mask allows only that type: $S(t) = \{\operatorname{tok}(\mathcal{D}[h_j])\}$.
Otherwise, if $r_j$ is known, the mask falls back to all domain-valid types: $S(t) = \{\operatorname{tok}(\tau) : \tau \in \operatorname{dom}(r_j)\}$.
At a \textsc{TailType} position, the processor has the relation type $r_j$ and head type $\tau^h_j$ from the prefix; valid tail types are determined by $\operatorname{range}(r_j)$, further restricted to the registered type if present in $\mathcal{D}$.

\paragraph{Implementation.}
Each $\tau \in \mathcal{E}$ maps to its first subword token ID under the model's tokenizer.
We verify these IDs are unique across all entity types (confirmed for both SciERC and DocRED with Qwen3-8B).
The mask sets disallowed first-token logits to $-\infty$.

\paragraph{Layer interaction.}
% exp_ids: phase2_scierc_semantic_only (DR 11.65%, RV 1.51%, ET 0.40%)
The semantic mask $S(t)$ is conjoined with the grammar mask $G(t)$; a token is permitted only if both layers allow it.
This conjunction embodies an \emph{enabling dependency}: semantic constraint lookup requires $r_j \in \mathcal{R}$, guaranteed only by the grammar layer.
Empirically, semantic-only constraints reduce DR by only 24\% (15.37\%\,$\to$\,11.65\%) and fail to eliminate RV or ET violations, whereas the full combination achieves 94\% DR reduction.
When $S(t) \cap G(t) = \emptyset$, the system falls back to grammar-only control (\S\ref{sec:composition}).


\subsection{Decoding Strategies}
\label{sec:strategies}

\paragraph{S1: Single-pass.}
Both layers are active throughout generation.
The entity registry populates as entities are generated and is immediately available for relation-level type checking.

\paragraph{S2: Two-pass.}\label{sec:s2}
The first pass generates only the entity list under grammar constraints.
Extracted entities compile an input-dependent grammar that additionally constrains head/tail names to match declared entities.
The second pass generates relations under this tighter grammar plus semantic constraints.

% exp_ids: phase2_scierc_full_s1 (DR 0.93%, R-F1 8.36, triples 1729, CF 9.90%)
%          phase2_scierc_full_s2 (DR 0.97%, R-F1 8.03, triples 1644, CF 8.03%)
\paragraph{Comparison.}
On SciERC, S1 and S2 achieve comparable DR (0.93\% vs.\ 0.97\%) but S1 yields higher R-F1 (8.36 vs.\ 8.03).
S2 does not resolve the CF trade-off from semantic enforcement.
We adopt S1 as primary; S2 is retained as an ablation.
